findings:
The paper (arXiv 2509.03563, Eq. 3 and 7-10, local PDF) differs from our port, which follows the authors' simulation code:
- the paper's nodes are kinematic projections of the cable line to the design altitude h_c, not integrated states;
- its damping is relative: between nodes, between node and robot, and friction relative to the formation centre;
- its command adds the expected acceleration and the cable tension f_i.
Our port damps absolutely against the pinned nodes, which is right only in hover, and adds the expected acceleration only when net_traj_lean is on.

Compute per tick (offline, this laptop):
- net.step: 2.2 ms;
- with the 21-node horizon preview: 48 ms, which the velocity loop never uses (it reads node 0 only), so turning it off changes no behaviour;
- velocity loop: 0.08 ms per 50 Hz tick.

new_files.purpose:
The card, written and frozen before any C twin run:
- question, arms, the one variable (mode), baseline (our planner at the frozen tree's defaults, not retuned);
- the port's deviations from Eq. 3/7-10;
- the fair-retune protocol: structures, search space, objective J, guards, time box, offline then twin;
- windows (hover [lift top + 1.5, + 61.5] s; line [PATH_START, + k*T]) and metrics;
- bars: a run is valid if it has no abort, drop or envelope fault before the window ends and at least 95 % of the window's rows; one re-fly per invalid run. A difference is stated only when all 3 runs of one arm beat all 3 of the other. Falsifier of 'our planner follows the path at least as closely': any cell where the network's 3 runs all beat ours on clock-free RMS or reach;
- runs and hosts, and a ## Critic section.

Offline retune harness, built from tools/velocity_probe.py's structure:
- tools/sil/plant.SilPlant at the C geometry with the rig thrust map and pack;
- the real DissipativeNetwork (incl. damp_relative and lean) at 10 Hz and the real VelocityLoop at 50 Hz with the tracker's offset and cap plumbing;
- an ema/slope/none velocity filter;
